    "hero_url": "https://dharmaweave.com/cdn/dharma-images/articles/heart-sutra/hero.jpg",
    "published": "2026-04-25",
    "body_md": "# Going Through Zero, Going Through Fear\n\n“Why the bats, Master Wayne?”\n“Bats frighten me, Alfred.”\n\nIn Buddhist thought, we don’t transform by avoiding fear. We transform by moving toward\nit—by standing close enough to feel its abyss without turning away. Fear of the dark. Fear of\nloss. Fear of death. Even fear of being invisible in this world.\n\nThis movement— toward rather than away—is at the heart of a short yet wildly significant\nBuddhist text known as the Heart Sutra.\n\nThe Heart Sutra does not begin with philosophy. It begins with a scene.\n\nOn Vulture Peak, the Buddha is present, seated in deep meditative absorption. He does not\nspeak. Nearby sits Avalokiteshvara, the bodhisattva of compassion, equally absorbed,\nlooking directly at the nature of reality itself.\n\nThis setting matters, because this teaching does not arise from explanation or dialogue. It\narises from feeling.\n\nAvalokiteshvara examines what Buddhism calls the five skandhas (or Pillars)—the\ncomponents that make up all experience and how it’s lived: the physical world, the\nsensations we feel, our perceptions, our mental formations like thoughts and memories, and\nconsciousness itself—the sense of being a witness. The Heart Sutra then makes its most\nradical claim: that form is emptiness, and emptiness is form.That the path toward something\nluminous and infinite —is through nothing, is through zero.\n\n![Avalokiteshvara amid stars and nebulae](https://dharmaweave.com/cdn/dharma-images/articles/heart-sutra/inline-1.jpg)\n\n\nI heard this mantra this winter in a monastery in Koyasan, where Japanese monks were\nchanting the core of the Heart Sutra:\n\nGate Gate\nParagate\nParasamgate\nBodhi Svaha\n\nRoughly this translates to: Going, going, going beyond, going completely\nbeyond—awakening, so be it.\n\nOur deepest fears are fears of loss—of people, of meaning, of identity, of the stories we tell\nourselves about who we are. To pass through emptiness is to risk losing all of that. The\njourney through zero is not philosophical. It’s akin to Batman embracing the very bats he\nfears the most.\n\nMahayana Buddhism allows all the possibility to truly experience all of creation in a single\ninstant. So if the infinite can be felt now—what prevents it? The answer the sutra gives is:\nEverything we are holding. To get to the infinite, to go through nothing, we must go through\nfear.\nThere is a story about a young mystic walking home one evening who looks up and sees\nbirds crossing a glowing sky at dusk. The sight is so overwhelmingly beautiful that his sense\nof self collapses. He faints and remains unconscious for days. This mystic was Ramkrishna\nParamhansa, the Guru of Vivekananda. In his moment of enlightenment, there were no\nwords and no intellect. Just beauty, infinite beauty—received fully.\n\n![Young mystic looking up at birds crossing a glowing sky](https://dharmaweave.com/cdn/dharma-images/articles/heart-sutra/inline-2.jpg)\n\n\nThis mirrors what the Heart Sutra points toward. Words can describe reality endlessly. They\ncan explain origins, structures, processes. But words always remain one step removed. It is\nthrough the Heart that true understanding emerges.\n\nWhen the mind is quiet—when attention is unoccupied—beauty can feel infinite. A single\ninstant can open completely.\n\nThe path to infinity, is through zero.\n\nListening\n\nWe’ve explored this mantra through sound as well—allowing repetition, rhythm, and silence\nto do what explanation cannot. You can listen to our musical interpretations of Gate Gate\nParagate Parasamgate Bodhi Svaha here:\nhttps://open.spotify.com/album/52mw5qowEYvOifazPWaH5V?si=_0IWnuFYQ5iHnmON5ovh\nsg\n\nhttps://open.spotify.com/album/3i2LlHZ7Aqah1MwhBb71uh?si=krIoUsPEQXqq8VT49g7TeQ\n\nGate Gate — keep going\nParagate — go beyond\nParasamgate — go completely beyond\nBodhi Svaha — awakening, grounded here\n\nToward the courage to pass through uncertainty. Toward the willingness to loosen names,\nroles, fears, and identities—just enough to see what remains.\n"
